## Why it runs twice

You're most likely running under **React.StrictMode** in development. In React 18+, StrictMode intentionally mounts each component, unmounts it, and mounts it again to surface side effects that aren't cleaned up. Your effect therefore runs twice. The empty dependency array is correct: it means "run once per mount," and StrictMode adds an extra mount in dev only. Production builds run the effect once.

You can confirm this by checking `index.js` for `<React.StrictMode>`.

This matters more than the extra log line. Your code pushes into a module-level `indexarray`, so both runs push data into the same array and you get **duplicate posts**. Also, mutating a non-state variable doesn't trigger re-renders, so the component relies on `setLoad` to re-render and happens to read the mutated array.

## The fix

Store the fetched data in state, and guard against stale or duplicate results with a cleanup function.

```jsx
import React from 'react';
import './App.css';
import { useState, useEffect } from 'react';
import Postspreview from '../components/Postspreview';

function Home() {
  const [posts, setPosts] = useState([]);
  const [isLoading, setLoading] = useState(true);

  useEffect(() => {
    let ignore = false;  // Set to true on cleanup so stale results are discarded

    async function load() {
      try {
        const res = await fetch('/api/posts'); // Replace with your DB query
        const data = await res.json();
        if (!ignore) {
          setPosts(data);
          setLoading(false);
        }
      } catch (err) {
        if (!ignore) setLoading(false);
      }
    }

    load();

    return () => {
      ignore = true;
    };
  }, []);

  if (isLoading) {
    return <div>This is loading...</div>;
  }

  return (
    <div>
      <div className="posts_preview_columns">
        {posts.map((post) => (
          <Postspreview
            key={post.id}
            username={post.username}
            idThumbnail={post.profile_thumbnail}
            nickname={post.nickname}
            postThumbnail={post.photolink}
          />
        ))}
      </div>
    </div>
  );
}

export default Home;
```

## Notes

- **The effect may still run twice in dev.** That's expected StrictMode behavior. The `ignore` flag ensures only the latest run updates state, so you won't see duplicates. If you truly need a single request, you can remove StrictMode, but the cleanup pattern is the better practice.
- **Keep data in state, not in a module-level variable.** Module-level variables are shared across all component instances and don't trigger re-renders.
- **Add a `key` prop** when mapping lists so React can track items efficiently.
- Your original snippet had a mismatched `})` before `},[]);`. That was probably a paste error, but make sure the dependency array is attached to `useEffect`, not placed inside its callback.

