## Why it happens

When you hydrate the whole document, React expects the DOM inside `<html>` to match exactly what `<App />` renders. Several things commonly break that.

1. **Extra nodes in the served HTML.** Your template probably looks like this:

   ```html
   <html>
     <head>...</head>
     <body>
       <div class="App">...</div>
       <script src="/static/js/bundle.js"></script>   <!-- not rendered by App -->
     </body>
   </html>
   ```

   The `<script>` tag, and any other markup you add around the `renderToString` output, isn't in your React tree. React sees an unexpected node and hydration fails.

2. **Whitespace and text between `<html>`, `<head>` and `<body>`.** Newlines or indentation in your template string become text nodes that React didn't render.

3. **Different content on server and client.** For example, the server renders `"Server says Hello World"` while the client renders `"Client says Hello World"`. Server and client must produce identical output on the first render.

4. **Browser extensions** that inject nodes into `<head>` or `<body>`, such as password managers or Grammarly. Test in an incognito window.

