## If you need head tags to change per page or per component

Use a head manager so the same logic works on server and client. `react-helmet-async` is a common choice:

```jsx
// App.js
import { Helmet } from "react-helmet-async";

export default function App() {
  return (
    <>
      <Helmet>
        <title>React App</title>
        <meta name="theme-color" content="#000000" />
      </Helmet>
      <div className="App">
        <h1>Client says Hello World</h1>
      </div>
    </>
  );
}
```

On the server, wrap the app in `HelmetProvider` with a `context` object, render, and then read the collected tags from `context.helmet` to insert into your template. On the client, wrap the app in `HelmetProvider` too. Helmet updates `<head>` after hydration, which is safe because it doesn't touch the hydrated `#root` content.

## Why this works

- The server returns complete HTML, and `#root` contains exactly what React renders.
- The client hydrates the same node with the same markup, so there's no mismatch.
- `<head>` is generated outside React's hydrated tree, which avoids the browser-versus-React differences that caused the error.

# Why hydration fails here

During hydration React walks the existing DOM and expects it to match, node for node, what `<App />` renders on the client. Any difference causes the error, including:

1. **Different content.** If the server markup says something like "Server says Hello World" and the client renders "Client says Hello World", that is a mismatch by definition. Server and client must render identical output.
2. **Whitespace and text nodes.** If your HTML template is pretty-printed, every newline or indent between `<html>`, `<head>` and `<body>` becomes a text node that React doesn't render, so hydration fails:
   ```html
   <html>
     <head>...</head>     <!-- the newline/indent before this is a text node -->
     <body>...</body>
   </html>
   ```
3. **Hydrating `<html>` while rendering `<head>`/`<body>` as fragment children.** If you render the whole document, let React own the whole document.

