Use **several focused effects**, one per concern, unless some side effects form a single synchronization unit. I can't see the code in your question (the images didn't come through), but this is the general guidance.

**Performance**

The difference is usually negligible, but separate effects are slightly better because each has its own dependency array:

```jsx
// Combined: re-runs both the search and the title update when either dep changes
useEffect(() => {
  document.title = `Results for ${query}`;
  fetchResults(query, page);
}, [query, page]);

// Separated: each runs only when its own dependencies change
useEffect(() => {
  document.title = `Results for ${query}`;
}, [query]);

useEffect(() => {
  fetchResults(query, page);
}, [query, page]);
```

With a combined effect, a change to `page` also re-sets the title, and the combined dependency list grows as you add logic. Separate effects avoid that unnecessary work and make cleanup more precise.

**Architecture**

- **Readability:** each effect states one purpose, so you don't have to read the whole body to know what it does.
- **Cleanup:** each effect returns its own cleanup, so unrelated teardown doesn't get tangled together.
- **Reuse:** a well-named effect can be extracted into a custom hook (`useDocumentTitle`, `useWindowResize`) and shared across components.
- **Order:** effects run in declaration order, so you can rely on that when one depends on another.

**When to keep them together**

Keep logic in one effect when it's a single synchronization that must stay paired. For example, subscribing and unsubscribing to the same source, or setting up and tearing down the same resource, belong together so the cleanup always matches the setup.

**Warning signs**

- An effect with many unrelated statements and a long dependency list: split it.
- Effects that only compute values from props or state: these usually don't need an effect at all. Derive the value during render or with `useMemo`.
- Effects that run in response to a user action: these usually belong in the event handler.

