**Use several `useEffect` hooks, one per concern.** The React docs recommend this approach, and it is better for both architecture and performance.

## Why multiple effects are better

**1. Dependencies stay precise (performance)**
Each effect re-runs only when *its own* dependencies change. Merging unrelated logic means everything re-runs whenever *any* dependency changes.

```jsx
// ❌ One effect: changing `theme` also re-subscribes to the chat room
useEffect(() => {
  const conn = connect(roomId);
  document.body.className = theme;
  return () => conn.disconnect();
}, [roomId, theme]);

// ✅ Separate effects: each runs only when it needs to
useEffect(() => {
  const conn = connect(roomId);
  return () => conn.disconnect();
}, [roomId]);

useEffect(() => {
  document.body.className = theme;
}, [theme]);
```

**2. Cleanup is correct and isolated**
Each effect has its own cleanup function, so tearing down one subscription doesn't disturb another.

**3. Readability and maintainability**
Related setup and cleanup sit together. You can read, change, or delete one behavior without touching the others.

**4. Easy extraction into custom hooks**
Separate effects can be moved into reusable hooks like `useChatRoom(roomId)` or `useBodyTheme(theme)`.

## When to combine them

Keep logic in a single effect when it is truly **one synchronization process**. That applies when the pieces share the same dependencies *and* conceptually belong together, such as setting up and configuring the same connection.

## Rule of thumb

Split effects by **what they synchronize with**, not by when they run. If two pieces of logic would make sense as separate custom hooks, they should be separate effects.

The performance cost of having multiple effects is negligible. The real cost comes from effects that re-run unnecessarily because unrelated dependencies were bundled together.

Split them up. One effect per concern is the pattern the React team recommends, and it's what I'd do almost every time.

## Why several effects is usually better

**Each effect gets its own dependencies.** This is the big one. If we put unrelated logic in one effect, its dependency array has to include everything that logic touches. Then a change to *any* of those values re-runs *all* of the logic.

```jsx
// One combined effect: changing `theme` also re-subscribes to the chat room
useEffect(() => {
  document.body.className = theme;

  const connection = createConnection(roomId);
  connection.connect();
  return () => connection.disconnect();
}, [theme, roomId]);
```

```jsx
// Split: each one runs only when its own inputs change
useEffect(() => {
  document.body.className = theme;
}, [theme]);

useEffect(() => {
  const connection = createConnection(roomId);
  connection.connect();
  return () => connection.disconnect();
}, [roomId]);
```

In the first version, switching themes tears down and rebuilds the chat connection for no reason. That's a real bug, and it gets worse as the effect grows.

**Cleanup stays simple.** Each effect returns its own cleanup function. We don't have to juggle several teardowns in one return.

**It's easier to read and move.** When an effect does one thing, you can see what it's for at a glance. You can also lift it straight into a custom hook later, like `useChatRoom(roomId)`.

## What about performance?

The cost of having more `useEffect` calls is tiny. React just keeps a list of them and compares dependencies after each render. You won't notice the difference between one and five.

The performance that matters is **how often your side effects actually run**. Splitting usually helps here, because each effect re-runs only when its own dependencies change.

## When combining makes sense

Keep logic in one effect when the pieces really belong together. That means they:

- depend on the same values, **and**
- must run together, or in a set order, every time.

For example, setting up a subscription and its matching cleanup belong in the same effect. Splitting those would be wrong.

## A quick rule I use

Ask: "If this value changes, should *all* of this code re-run?" If the answer is no, split it.

